[TG5 the construction-matched null — REBUILT AFTER ROUND-1 R1]
  ROUND-1 R1, OWNED IN FULL.  The original TG5 was defective
  TWICE OVER.  (i) Its actor extraction scanned each event tuple
  for the first single alphabetic character, which in transport
  events is the EVENT TYPE ('d','p','r','n'), never the actor — so
  it grouped by type on 100% of events.  (ii) Fixing that would
  NOT have rescued it: ANY null built as a disjoint union of
  totally ordered groups gives every element exactly ONE cover, so
  its maximum sky is 1 BY CONSTRUCTION and the gate could never
  fail.  That is the very D46f failure mode the gate existed to
  prevent, reproduced inside the prevention.  The null below is
  rebuilt so that it CAN fail.
  rewired null: max |directions| = {'A': 5, 'B': 7, 'C': 6}, decidable triples = 362, cover links matched = 7996
  [PASS] TG5(a) THE NULL IS CAPABLE OF FAILING THE COMPARISON — the defect R1 identified is repaired, not papered over.  A link-count-matched random DAG concentrates covers freely, and it in fact reaches 7 directions with 362 decidable triples, so a large sky in the null was a live outcome rather than an arithmetic impossibility.  The old union-of-chains null was capped at 1 BY CONSTRUCTION and could not have failed  (rewired null max = 7 (the old null was capped at 1 by construction), decidable = 362)
  [PASS] TG5(b) WHAT THE REPAIRED COMPARISON ACTUALLY SHOWS, reported in whichever direction it came out: transport's maximum sky is 4, the link-matched random null's is 7.  If the null MATCHES or EXCEEDS transport, then sky size is a generic consequence of carrier size and link count and NOT a property of the transport law — and this gate says so rather than asserting separation  (transport max = 4, null max = 7 -> NULL MATCHES OR EXCEEDS: sky size is GENERIC, not a transport property)

