[VERDICT — D47b]
  THE SKY IS AN ACTOR-WIDTH PHENOMENON.  Over 400 deterministic deep walks per width to depth 20, the largest sky never exceeds the actor width and grows only with it; at width 2 it stays at 2 no matter how deep the walk runs.  DEPTH CANNOT BUY WHAT ONLY WIDTH CAN.
  AND NO CEILING: TG2's plateau at 4 is a sampling-and-parameter artifact — a denser probe with SKY-B's depth varied reaches 5.  Growth is slow and is sensitive to the sky definition's own parameter; nothing here licenses a saturation claim.
  **THE ROUND-1 NULL REVERSAL.**  With the null rebuilt so that it CAN fail, it does the opposite of separating: a link-count-matched random DAG over the same carriers reaches 7 directions against transport's 4.  **TRANSPORT SKIES ARE NARROWER THAN CHANCE.**  The original TG5 claim (that cross-actor causation PRODUCES the sky) is WITHDRAWN; what the transport law does is CONSTRAIN the sky BELOW the generic value at matched carrier size and link count.  That strengthens the actor-width reading rather than weakening it — the bound is a real restriction, not an artifact of size.
  THE EXHAUSTIVE STRATUM IS UNDECIDABLE: max 3 directions against the 4 required, over three fully enumerated families.  Reported as undecidability, never as a 2+1 cap.
  THE SAMPLED STRATUM yields 44 decidable triples with 0 shattered 4-sets.
  WHAT MAY NOT BE SAID: no absence of shattering is evidence for 2+1 (pin §2), and D47a's SG3b is the empirical reason — real Minkowski skies fail the weaker instrument 22% of the time.  Circular-ones numbers above are DIAGNOSTIC and license nothing.
  THE PIN'S §5 PRE-REGISTERED EXPECTATION IS CONFIRMED, and the deliverable is the one pre-registered for that case: a validated instrument plus a CERTIFIED statement of the scale required — which TG2/TG6 now give in the right variable, ACTOR WIDTH.

